  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nOverall, the transition away from summer is appearing in the \nsynoptic pattern taking shape over the next week. Upper-level \nridging generally around the southern/central parts of the country\nwill still keep temperatures near to above normal in the broader \nregion. Split flow is noted in the guidance into next week, and \nthe southern jet stream around the periphery of the ridge will \ncarry embedded shortwave troughs and add more flow and shear to \nthe atmosphere. Very minor perturbations may bring a small chance \nfor showers or storms around Sunday/Monday, but the models \ndisagree whether ridging or troughing will be more established to \nallow much. Either way, the conditions through the weekend into \nearly next week will be drier without concern for humidity causing\ndangerous heat stress.\n\nBy the time we get to the middle of next week, the ensemble of\nguidance shows a breakdown of the ridge and a more full-latitude \ntrough swinging from the western CONUS toward the central CONUS. \nBeing a bit early in the transitional time of year, confidence on \nthe timing of these features is very low. Guidance ensembles are\nsettling in on a solution that brings less rainfall threat\nWednesday, but greater potential by the end of the week. We will\ncontinue to monitor this potential system as it marks a change \nfrom the stagnant summer pattern to a potentially more potent \ncool-season pattern. There will be significant subtropical \nmoisture related to east Pacific tropical cyclone activity that \ncould eventually support some locally heavy rainfall, but NCAR\nAI-NWP guidance remain bearish with any severe probabilities. \n/OAJ/EC/",
